//? Title: Test a repeated-measures effect when sphericity is doubtful
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare the Greenhouse–Geisser and Huynh–Feldt corrections

Eighteen participants complete the same task at four sessions in this
synthetic example. Repeated-measures ANOVA tests whether the four
session means differ. Sphericity requires all pairwise session differences
to have the same variance across participants. The spread of the later
scores suggests that this assumption may be doubtful.

```{cpp, id=repeated-anova, file="figures/repeated_anova_scores.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/repeated_measures_anova.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    linalg::DenseMatrix<double> scores(18,4);
    const std::vector<double> session{1,2,3,4};
    std::vector<double> mean(session.size());
    auto plot = graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t row = 0; row < scores.rows(); ++row) {
        const double id = row + 1;
        const auto pace = std::sin(id*.22), baseline = std::sin(id*.41);
        const double group = id <= 9 ? -1 : 1;
        const std::vector<double> values{
            5 + .9*pace + .42*std::cos(2*id),
            6 + .8*pace + .31*std::cos(3*id) + .33*baseline,
            6 + .7*pace + 1.3*std::cos(.9*id) + .6*group + .41*baseline,
            7 + .6*pace + 2.1*std::cos(.73*id) + .8*group + .25*baseline};
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < session.size(); ++j) {
            scores(row,j) = values[j];
            mean[j] += values[j] / scores.rows();
        }
        plot.line(session, values, "", graphics::RGBA{120,135,153,80}, 1.1);
    }
    const auto test = stats::repeated_measures_anova(scores);
    std::println("| Reference distribution | F | Epsilon | Numerator df | Denominator df | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto show = [&](const char* name, const stats::RepeatedMeasuresFTest& reference) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.3g} |", name, test.f_statistic,
            reference.epsilon, reference.df_numerator, reference.df_denominator, reference.p_value);
    };
    show("Uncorrected", test.uncorrected);
    show("Greenhouse–Geisser", test.greenhouse_geisser);
    show("Huynh–Feldt", test.huynh_feldt.value());

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.line(session, mean, "", graphics::RGBA{16,80,146}, 3)
        .title("Task scores across four sessions").x_label("Session").y_label("Task score")
        .x_ticks(session, {"1","2","3","4"}).size(720,420).save_svg("build/repeated_anova_scores.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Reference distribution | F | Epsilon | Numerator df | Denominator df | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Uncorrected | 11.232 | 1.000 | 3.00 | 51.00 | 9.04e-06 |
| Greenhouse–Geisser | 11.232 | 0.539 | 1.62 | 27.50 | 0.000572 |
| Huynh–Feldt | 11.232 | 0.588 | 1.76 | 29.97 | 0.000368 |
\image(figures/repeated_anova_scores.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Thin lines show individual participants. The thick blue line shows the session mean.)
\alttext(Eighteen score trajectories cross over four sessions. Scores range from about 4 to 7 in session 1 and from about 5 to 10 in session 4. The mean rises from about 5.4 to 7.4.)

Both corrections multiply the numerator and denominator degrees of
freedom by an estimate of epsilon from the residual contrasts. The F
statistic stays the same. Here, both estimates are below one, and each
corrected p-value is larger than the uncorrected value. Both corrected
tests still give evidence that at least one session mean differs.

[Greenhouse and Geisser (1959)|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02289823] · [Huynh and Feldt (1976)|https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986001001069]
